Thank you for the link. As described, the outsider gimmick puts me off the movie, as a Pole. Of course a visitor from the West would have felt very lost in 1981 Poland, even if Martial Law had not been declared - much like I found the US very alien when I first visited in 2011. It's just obvious for an outsider to be puzzled - there's nothing interesting about the premise. Also, what I dislike about dramatized films about Poland before 1989 is how they exaggerate the oppression and the bleakness. Life in general was much easier and more enjoyable for people then than movies make it seem. Of all the Eastern Bloc countries, Poland's regime was probably the least restrictive, and there was an element of fiction to it - neither the government nor the people really believed in any of the ideology. The government pretended it was communist, the people pretended they believed the government, and life went on.

(I wouldn't be surprised if it turned out American police kiII more people every year or at least every few years than the Polish "communist" regime did over it's 4+ decades)

